Which browser opens a link is a setting, not a hardcoded word
==============================================================

The browser pane said "Open in Brave" in three places and meant it as a constant. Upstream hands the
URL to `xdg-open` — whatever the desktop says — so the honest default is the system default, and the
honest way to change it is a control rather than a rebuild.

**The list is the desktop's own menu.** Every `.desktop` entry with `Categories=…WebBrowser…`, read from
`/usr/share/applications`, `/usr/local/share/applications`, `$XDG_DATA_HOME` and `$XDG_DATA_DIRS`. A
browser this source has never heard of appears because the desktop says it exists — that is how
FireDragon, Garuda's default, and Mullvad Browser get listed at all. Which one is the system default
comes from `xdg-settings get default-web-browser`.

**Three kinds of answer, all legitimate:** the system default (what stock QuixiDesk does), ask me each
time (a chooser per link; dismissing it opens nothing), or a named browser.

**The two ways of driving a browser are described rather than abbreviated.** Chromium-family browsers
can be driven by the agent while the user keeps their own window open; Firefox-family browsers are
started under a driver with their own profile. The picker says that in a sentence, because "CDP" and
"WebDriver" are the implementation's words and not the reader's.

**The wire follows the choice.** `open-external` accepts a `browser` in the request body, so the
chooser's answer takes the same path as the saved setting. A `.desktop` id launches through `gio` so
that entry's own `Exec=` is used; `system` short-circuits to `xdg-open`; a Chromium still gets `--app=`
so the page arrives as its own window instead of a tab inside a session already open.

**The tools icon is a saw blade** — a rim of hooked teeth on a circle with an arbour hole. The pencil
with a line through it it replaces read as "tools, disabled" rather than "tools".

